{"name":"Mauve Chalk","hex":"#e5d0cf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"},{"term":"chalk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A soft, white, powdery limestone (calcium carbonate, CaCO3).","A piece of chalk, or nowadays processed compressed gypsum (calcium sulfate, CaSO4), that is used for drawing and for writing on a blackboard (chalkboard)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply chalk to anything, such as the tip of a billiard cue.","To record something, as on a blackboard, using chalk."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chalk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve","wikidata":"Q604079"},{"title":"Chalk","blocks":[{"text":"Chalk is a soft, white, porous, sedimentary carbonate rock. It is a form of limestone composed of the mineral calcite and originally formed under the sea by the accumulation and lithification of hard parts of organisms, mostly microscopic plankton, which had settled to the sea floor. Chalk is common throughout Western Europe, where deposits underlie parts of France, and steep cliffs are often seen where they meet the sea in places such as the Dover cliffs on the Kent coast of the English Channel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Seven_Sisters_3.jpg/330px-Seven_Sisters_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalk","wikidata":"Q183670"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg/500px-Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg/500px-Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Nitzana Chalk curves\" situated at Western Negev, Israel, are chalk deposits formed in the Mesozoic era's Tethys Ocean","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANitzana_chalk_curves_(2)%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG/500px-Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG/1280px-Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG/500px-Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Open chalk pit, Seale, Surrey, UK","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASeale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Chalk_%28%22Upper_Chalk%22_Formation%2C_Upper_Cretaceous%3B_White_Cliffs_of_Dover%2C_England%2C_southern_Britain%29.jpg/500px-Cha